+# Stock reservations — specification
+
+## Why
+
+Checkout has to hold stock for a cart while the customer pays. When payment
+goes through, the hold becomes an order. When it doesn't, the hold is
+released. Carts get abandoned, so a hold nobody confirms must lapse on its own
+and free the stock. There is no background job, so expiry has to take effect
+without anyone calling a sweep. The cart service retries calls, so creating a
+hold must be idempotent and safe to repeat.
+
+## Interface (fixed)
+
+- `InventoryService.reserve(warehouse: str, items: dict[str, int], idempotency_key: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.confirm_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Order`
+- `InventoryService.release_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.get_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.available(sku, warehouse)` must take holds into account.
+- `inventory.models`: dataclass `Reservation` with `reservation_id: str`, `warehouse: str`, `items: dict[str, int]` (units held per SKU), `status: ReservationStatus`, `expires_at: datetime`, `order_id: str | None`; enum `ReservationStatus` with members `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`, `EXPIRED`.
+- `inventory.errors`: new `ReservationNotFound`, `ReservationExpired`, `InvalidReservationState`, `IdempotencyConflict` (all subclass `InventoryError`); reuse the existing errors where they fit.
+- `InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` keeps working as is.
+- Time comes only from the injected clock. There is no background job: expiry must take effect without anyone calling a sweep.
+
+Additions (my call, not fixed by the request):
+
+- The hold duration is an optional keyword argument to the `InventoryService`
+  constructor and defaults to 20 minutes.
+- `Reservation` and `ReservationStatus` are also exported from `inventory`,
+  like the other models.
+- The new errors carry the reservation ID (or, for `IdempotencyConflict`, the
+  key) as an attribute, the same way `OrderNotFound` carries `order_id`.
+
+## Definitions
+
+- **Hold**: units of a SKU in one warehouse set aside for a reservation.
+  Only an `ACTIVE` reservation holds stock.
+- **Expired**: a reservation counts as expired once
+  `clock.now() >= expires_at`, unless it was confirmed or released before
+  then. At exactly `expires_at` it has already expired.
+- **Available** (per SKU, per warehouse): on-hand, minus units allocated to
+  `PLACED` orders, minus units held by `ACTIVE` reservations. Never below 0.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Reserve
+
+1. `reserve` returns a reservation with status `ACTIVE`, `order_id` set to
+   `None`, and `expires_at` set to the creation time plus the hold duration
+   (20 minutes by default).
+2. Reservation IDs are sequential, starting at `RSV-0001`, then `RSV-0002`,
+   and so on. They are separate from the order ID sequence.
+3. Each requested line holds `min(requested, available right now)` units.
+   Any lost or expired holds are already excluded from "available".
+4. A line with nothing available is left out of `items` altogether. It is not
+   included with a value of 0.
+5. If no line can hold anything, `reserve` raises `InsufficientStock` and
+   creates no reservation. The error reports the first requested SKU with
+   `available=0`.
+6. Partial success is not an error: if at least one line holds units, the
+   reservation is created.
+7. Input is checked the same way `place_order` checks it, before stock is
+   looked at:
+   - an empty `items` raises `InvalidOrder`;
+   - a quantity that isn't a positive integer raises `InvalidQuantity`;
+   - an unknown SKU or warehouse raises `UnknownSku` or `UnknownWarehouse`.
+
+   Any of these failures creates nothing.
+8. While a hold is `ACTIVE`, it lowers `available` for its SKU in its
+   warehouse by the number of units held. This affects direct `place_order`
+   calls too: `place_order` cannot take held units and stays all-or-nothing.
+9. A hold in one warehouse never affects `available` in another warehouse.
+
+### Idempotency
+
+10. Suppose a `reserve` call comes in with a key that an earlier, successful
+    `reserve` used, and the same warehouse and the same *requested* items
+    (what was asked for, not what ended up held; the order of the items
+    doesn't matter). It returns that existing reservation in its current
+    state: `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED` or `EXPIRED`. It creates no new
+    hold, uses no new reservation ID, and does not change `expires_at`.
+11. A key reused with a different warehouse or different requested items
+    raises `IdempotencyConflict`, even if the original reservation has
+    finished. Nothing changes.
+12. Keys are shared across the whole service. They are not scoped per
+    warehouse.
+13. A `reserve` that fails does not use up its key. This covers
+    `InsufficientStock` and the input errors in criterion 7. A later call with
+    the same key is treated as a new request.
+
+### Expiry
+
+14. An `ACTIVE` reservation whose `expires_at` has been reached is treated as
+    `EXPIRED` everywhere, at that instant, with no sweep and no other call
+    needed:
+    - `get_reservation` reports `EXPIRED`;
+    - `available` no longer counts its units;
+    - `place_order` and `reserve` can take those units.
+15. `EXPIRED` is a final state.
+
+### Confirm
+
+16. Confirming an `ACTIVE` reservation that hasn't expired:
+    - creates an order with status `PLACED` from the normal `ORD-` sequence,
+      with `placed_at` set to the confirm time;
+    - gives the order one line per SKU in the reservation's `items`, covering
+      only the units actually held (after a partial hold, the order is
+      partial too);
+    - sets the reservation's status to `CONFIRMED` and its `order_id` to the
+      new order's ID.
+17. Confirming turns the hold into the order's allocation: `available` for
+    every SKU involved is the same just before and just after confirm.
+18. Stock is not checked again at confirm. If a write-off during the hold
+    left on-hand below what is held plus allocated, confirm still succeeds,
+    and `available` stays at 0 (the same as `adjust` already allows with
+    orders).
+19. Confirming a reservation that is already `CONFIRMED` returns the same
+    order (same `order_id`) and creates nothing. This holds at any time, even
+    after `expires_at` has passed.
+20. Confirming an expired reservation raises `ReservationExpired`, even if
+    the stock is still free. Nothing changes.
+21. Confirming a `RELEASED` reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`.
+22. The order created by confirm behaves like any other order:
+    `ship_order` and `cancel_order` work as usual. Cancelling it frees the
+    allocation, and the reservation stays `CONFIRMED`.
+
+### Release
+
+23. Releasing an `ACTIVE` reservation that hasn't expired sets its status to
+    `RELEASED` and frees its units from `available` immediately.
+24. Releasing a reservation that is already `RELEASED` returns it unchanged.
+25. Releasing an expired reservation returns it unchanged, with status
+    `EXPIRED`. It does not become `RELEASED`, and nothing is raised.
+26. Releasing a `CONFIRMED` reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`.
+
+### Lookup
+
+27. `get_reservation` returns the reservation's current state, including
+    expiry as described in criterion 14.
+28. An unknown reservation ID raises `ReservationNotFound` from
+    `get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation` and `release_reservation`.
+
+### Stock and ledger
+
+29. Reserve, release, expiry and confirm never write to the ledger and never
+    change on-hand. On-hand moves only through receipts, adjustments and
+    shipments.
+30. Holds never block `receive` or `adjust`. `adjust` still only refuses to
+    take on-hand below zero.
+31. `allocated()` still means units allocated to `PLACED` orders and does
+    not count holds. The stock report's `on_hand` and `allocated` columns are
+    unchanged in meaning. Its `available` column reflects holds, so it can be
+    less than `on_hand - allocated`.
+32. All existing tests still pass unchanged.
+33. The README's Concepts section describes holds, the reservation lifecycle,
+    and the new definition of available.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Extending, renewing or editing a hold. A retried `reserve` never pushes
+  `expires_at` back.
+- Caps on the number of active holds per cart, warehouse or SKU.
+- A background sweep job, or any reliance on one.
+- A separate "held" figure in the stock report or a `held()` query.
+- Holds that span more than one warehouse.
+- Expiring idempotency keys, or validating their format (any string is
+  accepted).
+- Concurrency and thread-safety. The service stays single-threaded and in
+  memory, as it is today.
+- Keeping previously returned `Reservation` objects up to date when a hold
+  expires. The current state is whatever `get_reservation` returns.
+- Persistence beyond the existing in-memory repository.
